+### §6.2 One-call decoding
+
+Capture Ref=(own A union Pending) minus FINAL-out IDs at reply-processing entry and keep it fixed as input for the entire call. Decode received cells minus Ref's encoding at their indices; every later rescue attempt similarly captures the current Ref at its start. The peeler's private working reference changes as it peels, not the captured Ref. Success means all residuals zero. Decoded items are algebraically consistent, never authenticated holdings. FetchBody and host validation remain the only authority for admission.
+
+After the arrival peel completes, eligible decoded positive items may start bounded fetches and thereby enter Pending. No prefix continuation occurs. peerCandidates=((Ref union decodedPositive) minus decodedNegative) minus peerExclusions describes a complete reconstruction only when no residual remains. Record this round’s item observations from the arrival outcome under §8, before later rescue attempts can discover more items. An item preference or counter alone never cancels a fetch or creates an epoch rejection mark. Partial positives still use the normal fetch path. Successful arrival replies release cell workspace after discovery/observations; ordinary incomplete replies retain indices, exclusions and ONE invariant residual array for dissemination/rescue. Core retains no second raw-cell array. Raw cells can be reconstructed as residual+Encode(current Ref); the harness separately owns wire bytes. Nothing is retained across round end or tip cancellation. Explicit contradictions discard the reply under the existing nonpunitive policy.
+
+For each retained reply, invariant residual=raw cells−Encode(current Ref) at its indices. A Ref membership gain/loss applies exactly one signed contribution at the item's scheduled indices in every retained reply. Update only net Ref membership: Pending-to-A or A-to-Pending transitions preserving membership have no delta. Admissions, removals, Pending changes, fetch failures and probation use this same rule. A decoded item never inserts R and never directly changes the invariant buffer; only the Ref change it actually causes does so.
+
+Each peel attempt copies the invariant residual into ONE transient working buffer of at most maximumSymbolsPerReply cells, peels that copy, then discards it. Charge the buffer within decode workspace. Never retain private peel subtractions as invariant residuals. Capture Ref at attempt start for peerCandidates and audit; after peel, apply eligible fetch/Pending effects. Partial positives follow the same live-fetch rules; they do not authenticate bodies.
+
+Maintain a dirty set ordered by poll order. A Ref change marks retained replies with touched scheduled indices. Repeatedly attempt the smallest dirty reply until none is dirty. A successful rescue reconstructs the peer raw/candidate view against that attempt’s Ref, discovers donors and fetches eligible items, then releases cell workspace. Rescue contributes no new item observations that round: neither decoded later items nor newly inferred shared items replace arrival abstention. This includes dirty-set attempts caused within the same API call after the first arrival peel. Already eligible arrival observations remain governed by their captured lifetime and the existing validity/discard rules. Ordinary failure leaves the reply retained and clean; contradictions follow §6.6. Cascading Ref additions can dirty earlier replies; choose the smallest dirty poll order next.
+
+Arrival observations use the existing bounded round-evidence records keyed to captured item lifetimes and selected peers. Record at most one outcome per selected peer/item; later rescue does not update it. Ordinary lifetime/validity filters still apply at scoring. No per-reply cell-index evidence buffer or extra evidence-only peel is retained or run. Capture/evidence storage is released at round end or tip reset and actual capacities remain charged under §6.5.
+
+No work cap and no proportionality claim. A retained reply is re-peeled only after a Ref change touches its indices. Within the cascade of one call, Ref gains at most P new Pending entries and an ID cannot re-enter Pending in that call. Assert attempts per call<=retained-reply bound*(1+Ref changes in the call), conservatively using K as the reply bound, without terminating at a runtime cap. Record residual-update touches separately from actual re-peel steps/attempts and rescued replies. No dirty reply remains when the call finishes. Admission and root changes do not abandon the round. Keep eligible item evidence and update retained residuals for net Ref changes, including admission/removal. There is no separate root-confidence state or root-change scoring veto. Tip/generation reset still cancels the round. Rescues use current Ref for dissemination; item observation eligibility and values come from the arrival outcome and the corresponding captured lifetime.
+
+Sizing estimatedCurrentDifference and estimatedRoundStartDifference are captured only at first processing, using its pre-peel bitmap and original attempt's lower bound; later attempts create no new samples and never rewrite earlier ones. The first peel counts as one attempt. A residual-delta audit record is per (Ref item,retained reply) pair, not per touched cell. Full-observation tests record the complete cascade in observation-owned growable storage (§10); audit allocation failure is a test failure, not a protocol ResourceLimit or an atomic-reservation requirement. Do not reserve a worst-case audit envelope. Protocol reservations and the attempt/no-dirty assertions remain mandatory.
+
+
+Inbound POLLs disseminate items only; their reference is own A, distinct from the (A union Pending) minus FINAL-out reference used when decoding received REPLY cells. They supply no batch vote, no per-transaction vote and no estimatedCurrentDifference/estimatedRoundStartDifference sizing sample: the responder did not select who polls it, and adversaries can poll at will. Use the existing transient working buffer for one attempt, charge its inbound per-attempt signed-claim bound2*M within the existing per-call claim accounting and observation attempt counters, then release all POLL-cell workspace. Inbound-triggered fetches use the existing P union reservation. No inbound poll slot is retained. Pending additions from an inbound POLL are ordinary Ref changes: update retained outbound residuals and run normal dirty-order rescue. These rescues disseminate items but do not create or upgrade this round’s item observations. Inbound polls themselves never gain evidence eligibility or sizing samples.
